  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/guides/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Manual Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/manual-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a manual and a curved treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They share only the absence of a motor. A curved treadmill has a concave slat belt on ball bearings — very low friction, no top speed, built for sprinting, and priced like a premium machine. A flat manual treadmill has a rubber belt sliding on a deck, high friction, a fixed incline, and is aimed at budget walkers. Confusing the two is the most common mistake in this category.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do manual treadmills have a fixed incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because gravity is what makes the belt possible to move. The frame is built as a wedge with the front raised so that the belt is helped downward and around the rollers. It is a structural requirement rather than a training feature, which is why you cannot set one flat — and why anyone with a calf, Achilles or knee issue aggravated by uphill walking should avoid the category.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a manual treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, not safely on a flat manual machine. The deck friction, the pulsing belt driven by discrete foot pushes, the short narrow running surface and the light frame all work against a running gait. These are walking machines. Running on a non-motorised treadmill means a curved slat machine, which is an entirely different and far more expensive product.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do manual treadmills burn more calories?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The effort is higher at a given speed because you are moving the belt and walking uphill, but the displayed figures are unreliable. Nearly everyone holds the handrails on these machines, which takes load off the legs and makes console estimates optimistic. Some of the effort you feel is the work of moving the machine rather than moving yourself. Wear a watch if you want real data.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a manual treadmill better than a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most buyers, no. A walking pad holds a steady pace, walks flat, needs no handrail and feels like a normal treadmill; the price gap has narrowed considerably. The flat manual wins when portability, silence, the absence of any outlet or the very bottom of the budget is the binding constraint — and those are real constraints for plenty of people.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do manual treadmills need maintenance?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Less than a powered machine but not none. The belt still slides across a deck, so it still needs lubricating, and arguably more attentively — here rising friction costs you effort directly rather than costing a motor. A dry manual treadmill becomes noticeably harder to walk on, which at least gives you honest warning. Belt tracking and tension also need occasional adjustment.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-18",
      dateModified: "2026-09-18",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
