        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A flat manual treadmill is a <strong class="text-white">legitimate product with a narrow
            use case, sold into a much wider one</strong>. It is cheap, light, silent, needs no
            outlet and has almost nothing that can fail — and it walks uphill, pulses underfoot and
            requires you to hold on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy one if portability, silence, the absence of electricity or the very bottom of the
            budget is your binding constraint. For almost everyone else,
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">an entry-level walking pad</a>
            is worth the extra money. And if you came here looking for the sprint machine from the
            gym, that is a
            <a href="/curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">curved treadmill</a>
            and this is not a cheaper version of it.
          </p>
